# Copy to .env at the repo root. `make` loads it for every target.
# These defaults line up with docker-compose.yml and are safe for local development only.

ENVIRONMENT=local

# docker-compose publishes Postgres 16 on 5433 to avoid a local install on 5432. If 5433 is
# already taken on your machine — another project's compose stack is the usual culprit, and
# the symptom is a container stuck in `Created` — change both of these to the same free port.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://rascal:rascal@localhost:5433/rascal

# Alembic only. In production this is Neon's *direct* endpoint while DATABASE_URL is the
# *pooled* one — a migration through a transaction-mode pooler fails confusingly. There is
# no pooler locally, so leave it unset and Alembic falls back to DATABASE_URL.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=

# 32 bytes, because anything shorter trips PyJWT's InsecureKeyLengthWarning on every call.
# Generate the production value with `openssl rand -hex 32` — see docs/DEPLOY.md section 3.
JWT_SECRET=0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef

# Object storage — Tigris, which is S3-compatible. See docs/tigris-setup.md.
# Tests still stub this with moto and presigning is offline, so the placeholders below are
# enough to run the suite; a real upload needs real values.
S3_BUCKET=rascal-media-dev
# Tigris is globally distributed and has no AWS-style regions. Leaving a real region here
# produces a signature error that reads like a credentials problem.
S3_REGION=auto
# https://t3.storage.dev from a laptop or CI; https://fly.storage.tigris.dev from inside Fly.
# Unset means real AWS, which is how this ran before Tigris.
S3_ENDPOINT_URL=https://t3.storage.dev
# Tigris keys look like tid_… / tsec_…. `tigris access-keys create` prints the secret once.
# A new key has NO permissions until you run:
#   tigris access-keys assign <id> --bucket <bucket> --role Editor
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=local-dev-access-key
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=local-dev-secret-key

# The `aud` claim every Apple identity token is checked against — an allowlist, comma-separated.
# One value is the normal case and is what production sets. A second is for a client that is a
# different audience for the same Apple credential, e.g. a browser signing in through a Services
# ID. A token whose `aud` is on the list passes; anything else is still rejected.
#
# Setting more than one value makes APNS_TOPIC required — see below.
APPLE_BUNDLE_ID=com.travisterry.childarchive

# --- Google sign-in --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated OAuth client ids, checked against a Google identity token's `aud`.
#
# **A separate list from APPLE_BUNDLE_ID and never the same values.** `aud` is a claim name the
# two providers share over entirely different namespaces; pooling them would let an Apple token
# for a Google client id verify.
#
# **Not a secret.** An iOS OAuth client is a public client — Google issues no client secret for
# it, which is why the flow uses PKCE — and this string ships inside every copy of the app.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=22574619282-vs702oo01a8vne7tsbc6h3ilk6a0sje6.apps.googleusercontent.com

# --- Mail ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `console` (default) writes the whole message to stdout and never opens a socket, which is
# what makes it impossible for a local run to send real mail to a real person. `resend` is the
# only other value and needs MAIL_API_KEY.
#
# This said `postmark` until Aug 17, 2026, which had been wrong since phase 60: D8 replaced
# Postmark with Resend and the adapter was deleted rather than disabled, so the name here named
# nothing. A stale example file is the one place a wrong value gets copied into a real .env.
# MAIL_PROVIDER=console
# MAIL_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=Pocket Chronicle <hello@rascal.local>
# Where replies go, if that is not MAIL_FROM. Unset means replies go to MAIL_FROM.
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# Where an emailed household invite points. Its own setting, for the same reason the magic
# link's base is one: the host mail links to and the host the API answers on are the same
# today and are not the same thing. The token is appended as a path segment — that is the
# shape the association file claims and the shape the app builds. No trailing slash needed.
# INVITE_LINK_BASE=https://rascal-api.fly.dev/invite

# --- The marketing signup (phase 60) ----------------------------------------------------------
# These three were missing from this file entirely, so a developer copying it got a
# `POST /v1/subscribe` that answers 503 with nothing here to suggest why.
#
# **Turnstile unset means the route refuses, and that is the design rather than an oversight** —
# no rate limit applies to this endpoint by the owner's call, so this is the whole protection.
# There is deliberately no local bypass: a key that skips verification when unset is the defect
# the phase was written to avoid.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
# The Resend audience the signup forwards into. There is no `subscribers` table — the list is
# provider-held — so this id is where a subscriber actually lives. Unset means the route refuses
# rather than accepting an address it cannot store.
# RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=
# Browser origins allowed to call the subscribe route, comma-separated. Never `*`. Defaults to
# BOTH the custom domain and the GitHub Pages origin, in that order — the custom domain landed
# with the Pocket Chronicle rename (Aug 27, 2026) and a browser on the apex sends that Origin,
# so it is an entry here rather than a replacement for the github.io one.
# **In production this is a Fly secret and overrides the default entirely.**
# MARKETING_ORIGINS=https://pocketchronicle.app,https://travist6983.github.io

# --- Double opt-in (phase 88) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the marketing site lives. Two consumers: the confirmation link sends the browser back
# here after a click, and the daily broadcast's footer links here. It replaced a hardcoded
# github.io URL in services/broadcast.py that went stale the day the custom domain landed.
# MARKETING_SITE_URL=https://pocketchronicle.app
# Where the emailed confirmation link points. Its own setting rather than derived, for the same
# reason EMAIL_SIGN_IN_LINK_BASE is: the host mail links to and the host the API answers on are
# the same today and are not the same thing.
# SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_LINK_BASE=https://rascal-api.fly.dev/v1/subscribe/confirm
# Short on purpose, and not for secrecy — the token is signed, so age does not weaken it. A
# confirmation link stays good for its whole life, so this is what bounds a stale link reopened
# after an unsubscribe. See services/subscribe_token.py.
# SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS=7

# --- The daily prompt broadcast (phase 69) ----------------------------------------------------
# **The switch that lets marketing email actually send, and it defaults to off.**
#
# Deliberately separate from the credentials above: MAIL_API_KEY and RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID exist to
# make POST /v1/subscribe work, and setting them must not also start mailing the list. Nobody
# setting a secret expects that.
#
# Off, `python -m rascal.jobs.broadcast` does everything except the network call -- picks the
# prompt, renders both bodies, writes its ledger row as `skipped`, and logs what it would have
# sent. ~~Open item 12: no marketing email before the P0 behavioural gate.~~ — **the gate was
# lifted by the owner on Aug 22, 2026; the flag is set on `rascal-api` and the broadcast has been
# sending daily since Aug 19.** It still defaults to off here, for the reason in the paragraph
# above rather than for the gate's.
# MARKETING_EMAIL_ENABLED=false
# The hour the daily prompt goes out, in BROADCAST_TIMEZONE. 8am, because a prompt that asks a
# parent to notice something today has already missed it by 8pm. The Fly machine runs HOURLY and
# this decides which run acts -- `jobs/notify.py` established that shape: hourly is the floor Fly
# gives, and a `--schedule daily` machine fires roughly whenever it was created. A run outside
# the hour writes no ledger row, because a row claims the day.
# BROADCAST_SEND_HOUR=8
# BROADCAST_TIMEZONE=America/Detroit

# --- The admin console's cost panel (phase 96, extended by phase 100) -----------------------------------------------
# All optional, and unset is the state this repository ships in. Each one missing produces a card
# in the console that names the variable and where the key comes from — never an error, and never
# a blank that reads as "$0 spent". Read-only tokens are enough; nothing in the console writes to
# a provider.
#
# **Three of the seven cards already work with what this repo has.** Tigris reads the same S3
# client media uploads through, Resend reads MAIL_API_KEY — deliberately not a second copy under
# a console-specific name, because one credential is one thing to rotate — and Apple needs no
# credential at all, because Apple publishes no billing API to call.

# An Anthropic *admin* key, `sk-ant-admin…`, from platform.claude.com -> Settings -> Admin keys.
# **Not a normal `sk-ant-api…` key** — the cost report rejects those, and the rejection reads like
# a permissions bug rather than a wrong-kind-of-key bug. **Admin keys do not exist on individual
# accounts**, so if Settings has no Admin keys section this card cannot work until the account
# becomes an organization. One of the two providers here that publishes a dollar figure.
# ANTHROPIC_ADMIN_KEY=

# `fly tokens create readonly`. Fly exposes no per-app spend over its API, so this card reports the
# machine inventory instead — which is more useful here anyway: it flags a machine whose image tag
# has drifted from the rest, which is the failure PR #57 spent a month invisible inside.
# FLY_API_TOKEN=
# FLY_APP_NAME=rascal-api

# github.com -> Settings -> Developer settings -> Fine-grained tokens, with Account permissions
# -> Plan -> Read-only and nothing else. NOT a classic token and NOT the repo-scoped token CI
# uses — this reads the account's plan, not a repository, and a token without Plan:read answers
# 404 rather than 403, which reads as a mistyped username. GitHub reports the amount itself, so
# this is the second card here with a real dollar figure on it.
# GITHUB_BILLING_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_ACCOUNT=travist6983

# console.neon.tech -> Account settings -> API keys, and the project's own Settings page. Neon
# reports consumption — storage bytes, compute seconds, egress — rather than dollars.
# NEON_API_KEY=
# NEON_PROJECT_ID=
# The organisation the project belongs to, from the console URL. Required by the v2 consumption
# endpoint and by nothing else: unset costs the daily compute chart and nothing more.
# NEON_ORG_ID=

# What object storage costs per GB-month, **in cents**, on the plan you are actually on. Unset
# means the card shows bytes and no estimate, which is the honest default: Tigris has no billing
# API, so any figure here is one somebody typed, and the card says so beside the number.
# STORAGE_PRICE_CENTS_PER_GB_MONTH=

# The Apple Developer Program membership, **in cents per year** — 9900 at the published US price.
# The only money Apple charges this project, and it is retrievable from no API: App Store Connect
# is free to call and publishes no billing endpoint. Unset means the card shows the pre-launch
# sentence and no figure.
# APPLE_MEMBERSHIP_CENTS_PER_YEAR=9900

# --- The studio's video render (phase 120) ---------------------------------------------------
# **Nothing to set. There is no key here any more, and that is the point.**
#
# Video used to be Google's Veo, one paid generation per shot, joined with ffmpeg — roughly a
# dollar fifty for thirty seconds, and GEMINI_API_KEY / GEMINI_VIDEO_MODEL /
# GEMINI_VIDEO_CENTS_PER_SECOND lived here. Phase 120 removed all three. Claude now writes a
# typed scene graph, the browser draws it with the same card renderer that draws a still, and
# the API re-encodes the recording to H.264 with the ffmpeg that is already in the image.
#
# If you have GEMINI_API_KEY set as a Fly secret, nothing reads it. Removing a secret from Fly
# is a separate manual act — `flyctl secrets unset GEMINI_API_KEY GEMINI_VIDEO_MODEL \
#   GEMINI_VIDEO_CENTS_PER_SECOND -a rascal-api`.

# How long a provider's answer is reused, and how long to wait on one. These are other people's
# rate limits; a dashboard left open in a tab must not poll six vendors on every render.
# BILLING_CACHE_SECONDS=300
# BILLING_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=12

# --- Looking at production from a laptop (phase 98) ------------------------------------------
# **A second connection, for the admin console's read routes and nothing else.**
#
# The obvious way to see live data is to change DATABASE_URL above. Do not. That variable is
# read by `make migrate`, `make seed`, `make reset-db` — which runs `alembic downgrade base` —
# and `make test`, which drops and recreates <db>_test. Aimed at Neon, every one of those is a
# command that destroys the archive, sitting in a Makefile one tab-completion away.
#
# Set this instead and only the console's read routes follow it. Everything else keeps talking
# to localhost, because nothing else learns the address.
#
# Getting the value: console.neon.tech -> your project -> Connection string. Paste it exactly
# as given; `postgres://`, `postgresql://` and `postgresql+psycopg://` are all accepted.
#
# **Use a read-only role.** In the Neon SQL editor:
#     CREATE ROLE console_reader WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'pick-something';
#     GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE rascal TO console_reader;
#     GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO console_reader;
#     GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO console_reader;
#     ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO console_reader;
# The connection also sets `default_transaction_read_only=on`, so Postgres refuses writes even
# with a full-access role. The role is the belt; that is the braces.
#
# While this is set the console is read-only: Library and Studio edits refuse, because they
# would save to your local database while showing you this one. Unset it to edit again.
# ADMIN_READ_DATABASE_URL=

# --- The social studio (phase 97) ------------------------------------------------------------
# **`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is NOT `ANTHROPIC_ADMIN_KEY` above.** Two different kinds of key from
# the same console and neither works in the other's place: the admin key reads organisation
# billing and cannot call /v1/messages; this one calls /v1/messages and cannot read billing.
# `scripts/generate_prompts.py` has read this same variable since phase 46, so a checkout that
# can already generate prompt candidates can already run the studio.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# The console's prompt-candidate generator (phase 103) reads its own model rather than the
# studio's, so moving one does not move the other. Sonnet by default and deliberately: a
# batch of candidates is volume a human filters on the spot, where the studio writes a
# handful of things that go out under the product's name. Both use ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
# ANTHROPIC_CANDIDATES_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# **Anthropic has no image or video endpoint on any model**, so pixels need a second vendor and
# no configuration of the key above will produce one. Unset means the Studio shows a card
# naming the variable rather than a button that fails. The brand-card renderer needs none of
# these — it draws on a canvas in the browser and exports PNG or WebM.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# REPLICATE_API_TOKEN=
# Version strings, not model names: Replicate pins by version, and a name would silently start
# rendering something else the day the model was updated.
# REPLICATE_IMAGE_MODEL=
# REPLICATE_VIDEO_MODEL=

# How many mission-library bodies may appear in social posts before the studio refuses.
# **A leak budget, not a rate limit.** D7 keeps the library out of anything public "whole or in
# generated slices" and calls it the one asset a competitor would want; posting prompts one at
# a time is how that happens without anybody deciding to. Raise it deliberately, having looked
# at the number the Studio shows.
# SOCIAL_FEATURED_PROMPT_CAP=24

# --- App Store Server Notifications V2 (phase 37a) --------------------------------------------
# All optional, and unset is the state this repository ships in. **Unset means the webhook
# refuses every notification with 401** rather than accepting an unverified one. There is
# deliberately no local bypass: the signature is the only thing standing between a stranger and
# somebody's billing state, and a key that skips verification when unset is the same defect
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY exists not to have.
#
# **Nothing here has ever been exercised against Apple.** No subscription product exists in App
# Store Connect; docs/blocked.md section 10 is the errand.
#
# The app's numeric Apple ID from App Store Connect — NOT the bundle id. Apple's library refuses
# to build a Production verifier without it, because a bundle id alone does not pin which app a
# signed payload is about. This one is readable today: the app record already exists, and it does
# not wait on the Paid Applications agreement.
# APP_STORE_APP_APPLE_ID=
# The bundle id a signed payload is pinned to. One string, never a list. Defaults to
# APPLE_BUNDLE_ID while that names exactly one audience, for the same reason APNS_TOPIC does.
# APP_STORE_BUNDLE_ID=
# Where the trusted Apple roots live, as DER .cer files. Defaults to the directory packaged
# inside the app, api/src/rascal/certs/appstore/ — see the README there, which carries the one
# command that puts Apple Root CA - G3 in it. Override only to point a test at its own store.
# APP_STORE_ROOT_CERTS_DIR=

# --- Push notifications (APNs) -------------------------------------------------------------
# All optional, and unset is the state this repository ships in: no APNs key exists yet, and
# it comes from the same Apple Developer enrollment as Sign in with Apple. See
# docs/blocked.md section 1a and docs/runbook-deploy.md section 4a.
#
# Unset means `build_push_client` returns a client that sends nothing and says so loudly at
# WARNING, rather than one that quietly reports success. All three are required together; a
# partial set is treated as unconfigured, because "nearly working" hides which piece is
# missing. Nothing here is needed to run the suite — it uses a stub.
#
# APNS_PRIVATE_KEY is the *contents* of the .p8, not a path to it.
# APNS_KEY_ID=
# APNS_TEAM_ID=
# APNS_PRIVATE_KEY=
# The APNs topic is one bundle id, never a list. It defaults to APPLE_BUNDLE_ID while that
# names exactly one audience. Set it if the two ever legitimately differ — a wrong value
# rejects every token with DeviceTokenNotForTopic.
#
# **Required once APPLE_BUNDLE_ID names more than one audience**, and the app refuses to start
# without it rather than picking the first one: silently guessing would be right today and
# wrong the day somebody reorders a comma-separated secret, which is a failure whose only
# symptom is that no push is ever delivered.
# APNS_TOPIC=

# --- Sign in with Apple revocation (phase 127) ------------------------------------------------
#
# Guideline 5.1.1(v) requires an app offering Sign in with Apple to call Apple's REST
# token-revocation endpoint when an account is deleted. That call is authenticated with a
# client_secret JWT signed by a **Sign in with Apple** key — a different .p8 from the APNs one
# above, created in the same Developer portal with a different capability. The errand is
# docs/blocked.md §24, and nothing here has ever been set.
#
# Unset means the revocation is skipped and said so at ERROR, and **the account is still
# deleted**. A parent who asked to be erased and was not, because a key was missing, is by far
# the worse of the two failures. All three are required together, like APNs.
#
# APPLE_SIWA_PRIVATE_KEY is the *contents* of the .p8, not a path to it.
# APPLE_SIWA_KEY_ID=
# APPLE_SIWA_PRIVATE_KEY=
# The client_id Apple's token endpoints expect: the audience the identity token was issued to.
# One string, never a list. Defaults to APPLE_BUNDLE_ID while that names exactly one audience,
# and is required once it names more than one — unlike APNS_TOPIC this does not refuse to
# start, it just leaves revocation unconfigured, because nothing here is configured anyway.
# APPLE_SIWA_CLIENT_ID=

# --- Instagram publishing (phase 104) -------------------------------------------------------
#
# The account is shared with the Rascal-Marketing repository, which holds the same two values
# as Actions secrets. ~~**One credential, two stores** — a refresh has to update both in one
# sitting or the other pipeline posts with a dead token.~~ — **struck Aug 22, 2026 (phase 111),
# and struck for exactly the reason docs/blocked.md §18 struck the same sentence: it is a
# one-store errand today.** Only the marketing repository holds a live copy — see the note
# below, which is the evidence rather than a restatement. **Un-strike it the day the API-lane
# publisher is armed**, because it becomes true again the moment IG_ACCESS_TOKEN is a Fly
# secret. The token lapses around 2026-10-19;
# see docs/blocked.md §18. (It said §17 until Aug 22, 2026. Commit a94d6f7, Aug 21, renumbered
# the Instagram entry from §17 to §18 and left this pointer behind — and §17 is now the resolved
# Gemini video section, so the pointer did not dangle, it quietly aimed at the wrong topic. That
# commit edited this file too, the Gemini block a hundred-odd lines above, which is the part
# worth remembering: a renumbering justified by "nothing anywhere referenced this one" had a
# reference in the very file it was already touching.)
#
# **Neither value is set on rascal-api**, verified against `flyctl secrets list` on Aug 22, 2026,
# and no deployed machine runs `rascal.jobs.social`. So today the two stores are this file and
# the marketing repository's Actions secrets; production is not a third one yet, and the refresh
# errand is correspondingly smaller than docs/blocked.md §18 describes.
#
# The account must be an Instagram *professional* account (Business or Creator). A personal
# account cannot be posted to by any API.
IG_USER_ID=
IG_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# **Credentials do not switch publishing on.** Setting the two above lets the console read the
# account; this is what lets it post. Same shape as MARKETING_EMAIL_ENABLED and for the same
# reason: setting a secret must never, by itself, start reaching the public.
SOCIAL_PUBLISH_ENABLED=false
